15:["$","section",null,{"className":"reference-section battle-flow-section","id":"battle-flow","children":["$","$L1d",null,{"guide":{"date":"August 26–28, 1862","place":"Bristoe Station, Manassas Junction, Bull Run Bridge, Kettle Run, and the First Manassas battlefield in Prince William County, Virginia","result":"Confederate victory in the Manassas Station Operations. Jackson captured and destroyed the principal United States supply depot at Manassas Junction, defeated George W. Taylor near Bull Run Bridge, delayed Hooker at Kettle Run, and withdrew to Stony Ridge before Pope could concentrate against him","facts":[{"label":"Episode scope","value":"The capture of Manassas Junction and Jackson’s move to Stony Ridge","note":"The narrative resumes at Bristoe Station on the evening of August 26 and ends after Jackson’s three divisions left Manassas Junction during the night of August 27–28."},{"label":"Campaign","value":"Northern Virginia Campaign","note":"The National Park Service groups the actions at Bristoe Station, Manassas Junction, Bull Run Bridge, Union Mills, and Kettle Run as the Manassas Station Operations."},{"label":"Lee’s objective","value":"Force Pope off the Rappahannock line before more reinforcements arrived","note":"Jackson’s movement against Pope’s railroad communications was intended to make the Army of Virginia turn back toward Washington. Lee and Longstreet followed Jackson through Thoroughfare Gap."},{"label":"Jackson’s position on August 26","value":"In Pope’s rear at Bristoe Station","note":"Jackson struck the Orange and Alexandria Railroad after marching through Thoroughfare Gap. Wrecked trains blocked the line, but other trains escaped and carried warning of the attack."},{"label":"Flank-march distance","value":"Approximately 55 miles in two days","note":"The NPS campaign account gives about 55 miles for Jackson’s August 25–26 movement. The episode describes his troops as exhausted after more than 50 miles."},{"label":"Night assault force","value":"About 500 men from the 21st Georgia and 21st North Carolina","note":"Brig. Gen. Isaac R. Trimble volunteered his two regiments. Jackson sent Maj. Gen. J. E. B. Stuart and cavalry to reinforce them and take command of the expedition."},{"label":"Manassas garrison","value":"A small mixed force under Capt. Samuel M. Craig","note":"The episode identifies 115 men of the 105th Pennsylvania, eight guns of the 11th New York Battery, and inexperienced troopers of the 12th Pennsylvania Cavalry. Craig treated the first reports as a guerrilla or cavalry raid."},{"label":"Capture of the junction","value":"The garrison was routed in less than five minutes","note":"The attack came after midnight on August 26–27. The episode gives more than 300 United States prisoners and six captured guns, with two Confederates killed and two wounded."},{"label":"Captured stores","value":"More than 100 loaded boxcars and extensive depot supplies in the episode’s account","note":"The stores included food, clothing, equipment, ammunition, horses, ambulances, and sutler goods. Jackson’s hungry troops consumed or carried off what they could."},{"label":"Morning deployment","value":"Hill and Taliaferro at Manassas; Ewell remained at Bristoe","note":"Jackson moved A. P. Hill’s and William B. Taliaferro’s divisions to the junction at first light on August 27. Richard S. Ewell’s three remaining brigades screened Jackson’s rear along the railroad."},{"label":"Waagner’s probe","value":"The 2nd New York Heavy Artillery approached from Centreville","note":"Col. Gustavus Waagner crossed Bull Run at Mitchell’s Ford with soldiers serving as infantry and joined surviving artillerymen from the depot garrison. He withdrew when Hill deployed a much larger force."},{"label":"Hill’s display of force","value":"About 9,000 men and 28 guns in the episode’s account","note":"Jackson ordered Hill’s division east of Manassas Junction after hearing the artillery exchange. Waagner did not continue his advance against that line."},{"label":"Taylor’s mission and advance","value":"Guard Bull Run Bridge and a railroad repair train","note":"Taylor instead advanced the 1st, 2nd, and 3rd New Jersey toward Manassas Junction, believing that he faced a raiding party. He left the 4th New Jersey at the bridge to await the 11th and 12th Ohio."},{"label":"Bull Run Bridge action","value":"Hill held fire until Taylor was within about 300 yards","note":"Artillery and infantry fire stopped the New Jersey advance. Jackson approached under a white flag to request surrender, but Taylor continued the action and then ordered a withdrawal."},{"label":"Taylor brigade casualties","value":"About 200 captured and at least 150 killed or wounded in the episode’s account","note":"The retreat became a rout at the railroad bridge. The 11th and 12th Ohio covered survivors. Taylor was mortally wounded; NPS describes the total loss as several hundred casualties."},{"label":"Reinforcement decision at Alexandria","value":"McClellan held Franklin’s and Sumner’s corps near Washington","note":"After Taylor’s unsupported defeat, McClellan stated that corps should not advance without their artillery and cavalry. He countermanded Franklin’s movement and kept Sumner at Alexandria."},{"label":"Pope’s response","value":"Concentrate at Gainesville and destroy Jackson east of the Bull Run Mountains","note":"By midnight on August 26, Pope understood that Jackson had turned his right. His August 27 orders sought to place his army between Jackson and Longstreet while Army of the Potomac troops approached from the east."},{"label":"Kettle Run","value":"Hooker attacked Ewell’s rear guard during the afternoon of August 27","note":"Hooker advanced about ten miles in the heat with limited ammunition and little artillery. Ewell burned the Kettle Run bridge, resisted for several hours, and retired toward Manassas after receiving Jackson’s permission."},{"label":"Kettle Run losses","value":"About 600 total casualties according to NPS","note":"The episode assigns more than 300 losses to Hooker, including roughly half of the 72nd New York, and fewer than 200 to Ewell. Historical totals vary by the boundaries used for the action."},{"label":"Disposition of the depot","value":"Useful stores were issued or removed; the remainder was burned","note":"Jackson directed surgeons to take ambulances and medical supplies, mounted troops to take horses, and infantry to carry four days’ rations. Liquor was destroyed to prevent drunkenness."},{"label":"Decision to evacuate","value":"Union pressure made Manassas Junction untenable before Longstreet arrived","note":"Waagner and Taylor approached from the east while Hooker pressed Ewell from the southwest. Jackson began his withdrawal late on August 27 and ordered the remaining depot property destroyed."},{"label":"Night movement and Stony Ridge","value":"Three divisions moved toward the old First Manassas battlefield","note":"Taliaferro took the direct route. Hill and Ewell mistakenly moved toward Centreville before backtracking. Jackson selected wooded high ground north of Groveton, where an unfinished railroad grade provided cover."},{"label":"Operational result","value":"Jackson drew Pope away from the Rappahannock and avoided encirclement","note":"The depot was destroyed, Pope’s railroad communications were disrupted, and Jackson remained intact on the old battlefield. The move set the conditions for the opening fight at Brawner Farm on August 28."},{"label":"Recommended book","value":"“A Self-Made Man: The Political Life of Abraham Lincoln, 1809–1849” by Sidney Blumenthal","note":"The hosts identify the book as unrelated to Second Manassas but recommend its treatment of Lincoln’s early political career."},{"label":"Transcript normalizations","value":"Taliaferro, Ewell, Heintzelman, Waagner, Haupt, sutlers, and Rhine wine","note":"The transcript produces “Tolliver,” “Yule,” “Heinselman,” “Wagner,” “Hopped,” “settlers,” and “rind wine.” It also says “Leon Longstreet” once; the intended name is James Longstreet."}],"maps":[{"title":"Jackson behind Pope’s army","caption":"Jackson crossed the Bull Run Mountains through Thoroughfare Gap, struck the Orange and Alexandria Railroad at Bristoe Station, and reached Manassas Junction. Lee and Longstreet followed while United States formations moved toward the threatened railroad. Routes, terrain, and distances are generalized. This map is schematic and not to scale.","source":"National Park Service, U.S. Army Center of Military History, Jedediah Hotchkiss campaign map, and episode transcript; see sources below.","image":"/images/ep175-operational-context.svg","alt":"Schematic map of Jackson’s flank march to Bristoe Station and Manassas Junction, with Longstreet following and United States forces concentrating."},{"title":"Manassas Station operations and the night withdrawal","caption":"Hill faced Waagner and Taylor east of Manassas Junction while Ewell delayed Hooker at Kettle Run. During the night, Jackson’s divisions left the burning depot for the old battlefield. Positions, routes, and troop frontages are generalized. This map is schematic and not to scale.","source":"National Park Service, Official Records, Jedediah Hotchkiss campaign map, and episode transcript; see sources below.","image":"/images/ep175-manassas-operations.svg","alt":"Schematic map of the August 27 fighting at Bull Run Bridge and Kettle Run and Jackson’s movement toward Stony Ridge."},{"title":"Damaged rolling stock at Manassas Junction","caption":"Timothy H. O’Sullivan photographed United States soldiers beside damaged Orange and Alexandria Railroad rolling stock at Manassas Junction in August 1862. The photograph records the railroad landscape during the campaign but does not depict the nighttime assault itself.","source":"Library of Congress Prints and Photographs Division; see sources below.","image":"/images/ep175-manassas-rolling-stock.jpg","alt":"United States soldiers standing beside damaged railroad rolling stock at Manassas Junction in August 1862."}],"moments":[{"time":"4:16","audioTime":256,"title":"The episode returns to Bristoe Station","detail":"Jackson is behind Pope’s army on the evening of August 26 after cutting the Orange and Alexandria Railroad."},{"time":"4:47","audioTime":287,"title":"Manassas Junction is the next objective","detail":"The depot is four or five miles away, but Jackson’s men have marched more than 50 miles in two days."},{"time":"4:58","audioTime":298,"title":"Trimble volunteers to take the depot","detail":"Isaac R. Trimble proposes an assault by the 21st Georgia and 21st North Carolina."},{"time":"5:11","audioTime":311,"title":"Stuart reinforces the night expedition","detail":"Jackson sends Stuart’s cavalry to support Trimble’s approximately 500 infantry and take command."},{"time":"5:29","audioTime":329,"title":"Craig’s garrison is described","detail":"Samuel M. Craig holds Manassas Junction with a small mixed force of infantry, artillery, and cavalry."},{"time":"6:17","audioTime":377,"title":"Stuart drives in the pickets","detail":"Confederate cavalry reaches the outer guard first, then pauses while Trimble’s infantry comes up."},{"time":"6:51","audioTime":411,"title":"Trimble attacks after the guard stands down","detail":"The Confederate infantry strikes from the darkness and routes the depot garrison in less than five minutes."},{"time":"7:02","audioTime":422,"title":"Prisoners and artillery are captured","detail":"The episode counts more than 300 prisoners and six guns, with four Confederate casualties."},{"time":"7:10","audioTime":430,"title":"The scale of the depot becomes clear","detail":"More than 100 boxcars and warehouses contain military stores, food, ammunition, equipment, and sutler goods."},{"time":"8:43","audioTime":523,"title":"Jackson divides the morning tasks","detail":"Hill and Taliaferro move to Manassas Junction while Ewell remains at Bristoe Station to watch Pope."},{"time":"10:16","audioTime":616,"title":"Waagner approaches from Centreville","detail":"The 2nd New York Heavy Artillery crosses Bull Run and joins surviving depot artillerymen."},{"time":"10:32","audioTime":632,"title":"Hill deploys east of the junction","detail":"Jackson sends the full division to support Stuart; the episode gives 9,000 men and 28 guns. Waagner withdraws."},{"time":"11:59","audioTime":719,"title":"Taylor’s brigade moves toward Bull Run Bridge","detail":"The railroad mission is intended to secure the bridge and protect a repair train, not attack Jackson’s main force."},{"time":"12:44","audioTime":764,"title":"Taylor advances beyond his assigned position","detail":"He leaves the 4th New Jersey at the bridge and leads the 1st, 2nd, and 3rd New Jersey toward Manassas Junction."},{"time":"13:48","audioTime":828,"title":"Hill waits for close range","detail":"Jackson orders the Confederate guns to hold fire until Taylor’s line is within about 300 yards."},{"time":"14:07","audioTime":847,"title":"Jackson requests Taylor’s surrender","detail":"Jackson rides forward under a white flag after artillery and musketry stop the attack. A United States shot ends the attempt."},{"time":"14:33","audioTime":873,"title":"The bridge constricts Taylor’s retreat","detail":"The New Jersey regiments withdraw in order at first, then crowd at the bridge and break under Confederate artillery fire."},{"time":"14:52","audioTime":892,"title":"Taylor’s losses are counted","detail":"The episode gives about 200 captured and at least 150 killed or wounded. Taylor is mortally wounded."},{"time":"16:29","audioTime":989,"title":"Franklin and Sumner remain near Washington","detail":"McClellan countermanded Franklin’s movement and kept Sumner at Alexandria, preventing either corps from reaching Pope in time."},{"time":"18:14","audioTime":1094,"title":"Pope recognizes that his right has been turned","detail":"He correctly places Jackson near Manassas, Longstreet near Thoroughfare Gap, and the rest of Lee’s force farther west."},{"time":"18:59","audioTime":1139,"title":"Pope orders a concentration at Gainesville","detail":"The Army of Virginia is directed to close on Jackson east of the Bull Run Mountains before Longstreet can join him."},{"time":"19:41","audioTime":1181,"title":"Hooker advances toward Bristoe Station","detail":"The 72nd New York reconnoiters by train, then Hooker pushes the rest of his division toward Kettle Run."},{"time":"20:25","audioTime":1225,"title":"Ewell forms across the railroad","detail":"Three Confederate brigades deploy to screen Jackson’s rear and protect the route back to Manassas Junction."},{"time":"20:41","audioTime":1241,"title":"Hooker pushes Ewell across Kettle Run","detail":"At about 3 p.m., the United States advance takes the bridge area; Ewell’s men burn the bridge as they withdraw."},{"time":"21:15","audioTime":1275,"title":"The Kettle Run fight remains balanced for about an hour","detail":"Hooker presses despite heat, fatigue, limited ammunition, and missing artillery. His numerical advantage then forces Ewell back."},{"time":"21:48","audioTime":1308,"title":"Ewell receives permission to retire","detail":"Jackson’s order allows Ewell to fall back toward Manassas before his retreat is cut off."},{"time":"21:55","audioTime":1315,"title":"Kettle Run casualties are assessed","detail":"The episode gives more than 300 United States losses and fewer than 200 Confederate losses; NPS summarizes about 600 total."},{"time":"22:12","audioTime":1332,"title":"Jackson distributes captured property","detail":"Surgeons take medical supplies and ambulances, mounted troops take horses, and infantry receive food from the depot."},{"time":"22:51","audioTime":1371,"title":"Jackson decides that the depot cannot be held","detail":"Pressure from the east and southwest is increasing while Longstreet remains too far away. Jackson begins removing useful stores."},{"time":"25:33","audioTime":1533,"title":"The remaining stores are prepared for destruction","detail":"As the divisions leave late on August 27, boxcars, warehouses, and ammunition that cannot be moved are set on fire."},{"time":"26:15","audioTime":1575,"title":"Jackson selects the old battlefield","detail":"The three divisions are ordered about seven miles north toward the First Manassas field to wait for Longstreet."},{"time":"26:29","audioTime":1589,"title":"Separate routes obscure the withdrawal","detail":"Taliaferro moves directly, while Hill and Ewell take wrong turns toward Centreville before correcting their routes."},{"time":"27:24","audioTime":1644,"title":"Jackson chooses Stony Ridge","detail":"The wooded ridge north of Groveton conceals his troops, and the unfinished railroad grade offers defensive cover."},{"time":"28:30","audioTime":1710,"title":"The book recommendation begins","detail":"The hosts recommend Sidney Blumenthal’s “A Self-Made Man,” the first volume of his political biography of Abraham Lincoln."}],"command":[{"side":"United States","color":"#315f8c","branches":[{"commander":"Maj. Gen. John Pope","command":"Army of Virginia","subordinates":"Ordered his scattered commands to concentrate toward Gainesville and Manassas"},{"commander":"Maj. Gen. Henry W. Halleck","command":"General-in-Chief","subordinates":"Coordinated from Washington but did not produce a unified advance by Pope and the arriving Army of the Potomac corps"},{"commander":"Maj. Gen. George B. McClellan","command":"Army of the Potomac at Alexandria","subordinates":"Held Franklin’s VI Corps and Sumner’s II Corps until their artillery and cavalry were available"},{"commander":"Maj. Gen. Samuel P. Heintzelman","command":"III Corps, Army of the Potomac","subordinates":"Hooker’s division fought at Kettle Run; Kearny’s division moved separately toward Greenwich"},{"commander":"Maj. Gen. Joseph Hooker","command":"2nd Division, III Corps","subordinates":"Advanced toward Bristoe Station and forced Ewell to retire after the Kettle Run action"},{"commander":"Brig. Gen. George W. Taylor","command":"1st New Jersey Brigade, VI Corps","subordinates":"Advanced three New Jersey regiments from Bull Run Bridge without artillery or cavalry support and was mortally wounded"},{"commander":"Col. Gustavus Waagner","command":"2nd New York Heavy Artillery","subordinates":"Probed west from Centreville and withdrew after Hill displayed a much larger force"}]},{"side":"Confederacy","color":"#9a3d32","branches":[{"commander":"Gen. Robert E. Lee","command":"Army of Northern Virginia","subordinates":"Sent Jackson against Pope’s railroad communications and accompanied Longstreet through Thoroughfare Gap"},{"commander":"Maj. Gen. Thomas J. “Stonewall” Jackson","command":"Jackson’s wing","subordinates":"Directed the capture, resupply, destruction, and withdrawal at Manassas Junction"},{"commander":"Maj. Gen. A. P. Hill","command":"Light Division","subordinates":"Deployed east of Manassas Junction against Waagner and Taylor"},{"commander":"Maj. Gen. Richard S. Ewell","command":"Ewell’s division","subordinates":"Held three brigades at Bristoe, delayed Hooker, and withdrew when authorized"},{"commander":"Brig. Gen. William B. Taliaferro","command":"Taliaferro’s division","subordinates":"Moved to the depot and took the direct route to the old battlefield that night"},{"commander":"Brig. Gen. Isaac R. Trimble","command":"4th Brigade, Ewell’s division","subordinates":"Led the 21st Georgia and 21st North Carolina in the nighttime capture"},{"commander":"Maj. Gen. J. E. B. Stuart","command":"Cavalry supporting Jackson","subordinates":"Took command of the depot expedition, drove in pickets, and screened Hill"}]}],"people":[{"name":"Thomas J. “Stonewall” Jackson","role":"Confederate wing commander who captured and destroyed Manassas Junction and moved to Stony Ridge","side":"Confederacy","image":"/images/ep175-stonewall-jackson.jpg"},{"name":"Joseph Hooker","role":"United States division commander who attacked Ewell at Kettle Run","side":"United States","image":"/images/ep175-joseph-hooker.jpg"},{"name":"George W. Taylor","role":"New Jersey brigade commander mortally wounded after advancing beyond Bull Run Bridge","side":"United States"},{"name":"John Pope","role":"Army of Virginia commander who ordered a concentration against Jackson","side":"United States"},{"name":"George B. McClellan","role":"Army of the Potomac commander who held two corps near Alexandria","side":"United States"},{"name":"Henry W. Halleck","role":"United States general-in-chief coordinating the response from Washington","side":"United States"},{"name":"Samuel P. Heintzelman","role":"III Corps commander whose Hooker division fought at Kettle Run","side":"United States"},{"name":"Gustavus Waagner","role":"2nd New York Heavy Artillery commander who probed toward Manassas Junction","side":"United States"},{"name":"Samuel M. Craig","role":"105th Pennsylvania captain commanding the small depot garrison","side":"United States"},{"name":"Robert E. Lee","role":"Army of Northern Virginia commander who sent Jackson around Pope and followed with Longstreet","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"James Longstreet","role":"Confederate wing commander following Jackson’s route through Thoroughfare Gap","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"A. P. Hill","role":"Light Division commander whose troops defeated Taylor east of the junction","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"Richard S. Ewell","role":"Division commander who screened Jackson and delayed Hooker at Kettle Run","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"William B. Taliaferro","role":"Division commander who moved directly from the junction to the old battlefield","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"Isaac R. Trimble","role":"Brigade commander who proposed and led the infantry assault on the depot","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"J. E. B. Stuart","role":"Cavalry commander who supported Trimble and screened the eastern approaches","side":"Confederacy"}],"losses":[{"name":"Brig. Gen. George W. Taylor","side":"United States","role":"Commander, 1st New Jersey Brigade, VI Corps","outcome":"Mortally wounded during the retreat from Hill’s line near Bull Run Bridge on August 27. He was removed from the field toward Alexandria and died from the wound several days later."}],"gallery":[{"image":"/images/ep175-operational-context.svg","alt":"Schematic regional map of Jackson’s movement to Manassas Junction and the United States response.","caption":"Jackson’s march through Thoroughfare Gap put him behind Pope’s Rappahannock line. Lee and Longstreet followed while United States forces turned toward the railroad.","source":"National Park Service, U.S. Army Center of Military History, Jedediah Hotchkiss campaign map, and episode transcript; see sources below."},{"image":"/images/ep175-manassas-operations.svg","alt":"Three-panel schematic of the junction, Kettle Run, and Jackson’s withdrawal.","caption":"Hill defeated Taylor, Ewell delayed Hooker, and Jackson’s divisions left the depot during the night. The diagram is generalized and not to scale.","source":"National Park Service, Official Records, Jedediah Hotchkiss campaign map, and episode transcript; see sources below."},{"image":"/images/ep175-manassas-rolling-stock.jpg","alt":"United States soldiers beside damaged Orange and Alexandria Railroad rolling stock at Manassas Junction in August 1862.","caption":"O’Sullivan’s August 1862 photograph records damaged railroad property at the junction, not the assault in progress.","source":"Library of Congress Prints and Photographs Division; see sources below."},{"image":"/images/ep175-stonewall-jackson.jpg","alt":"Nineteenth-century engraving of Thomas J. Stonewall Jackson based on a photograph from life.","caption":"Jackson directed the capture and destruction of the depot, then moved his three divisions to concealed ground near Stony Ridge.","source":"Library of Congress Prints and Photographs Division; see sources below."},{"image":"/images/ep175-joseph-hooker.jpg","alt":"Civil War glass-negative portrait of United States general Joseph Hooker in uniform.","caption":"Hooker advanced along the railroad and forced Ewell’s rear guard to retire from Kettle Run.","source":"Library of Congress Prints and Photographs Division; see sources below."}],"sources":[{"label":"Official Civil War Podcast episode 175 page","url":"https://civilwarpodcast.org/2016/12/18/civil-war-podcast-episode-175/"},{"label":"National Park Service battle summary: Manassas Station Operations","url":"https://www.nps.gov/civilwar/search-battles-detail.htm?battleCode=va024"},{"label":"Manassas National Battlefield Park campaign account: August 28, 1862","url":"https://www.nps.gov/articles/august-28-1862.htm"},{"label":"Manassas National Battlefield Park: The Confederates Take August","url":"https://www.nps.gov/articles/the-confederates-take-august.htm"},{"label":"National Park Service biography: George W. Taylor","url":"https://www.nps.gov/civilwar/search-battles-person-detail.htm?personId=F02C1113-80D3-40A9-99D0-71F90E30D2CE"},{"label":"U.S. Army Center of Military History: The Battle of Second Manassas, self-guided tour","url":"https://history.army.mil/Publications/Publications-Catalog/The-Battle-of-Second-Manassas-Self-Guided-Tour/"},{"label":"Official Records, Series I, Volume XII, Part II","url":"https://ehistory.osu.edu/books/official-records/016"},{"label":"Primary map: Jedediah Hotchkiss campaign map, August 26–September 2, 1862","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2005625017/"},{"label":"Library of Congress: O’Sullivan photograph of damaged rolling stock at Manassas Junction","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2018666231/"},{"label":"Library of Congress: “Stonewall” Jackson engraving from a photograph from life","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2003674500/"},{"label":"Library of Congress: Joseph Hooker glass-negative portrait","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2018669830/"},{"label":"Official podcast book-recommendations list","url":"https://civilwarpodcast.org/book-recommendations/"}]}}]}]
